    "type": "open_ended",
    "difficulty": "basic",
    "question": "What is the difference between a hard inquiry and a soft inquiry on your credit report?",
    "reference": "A hard inquiry occurs when a lender checks your credit as part of a lending decision (e.g., applying for a mortgage, credit card, or auto loan). Hard inquiries can temporarily lower your score by a few points and remain on your report for 2 years. A soft inquiry occurs when you check your own credit, when a lender pre-approves you, or for background checks. Soft inquiries do not affect your credit score at all.",
    "candidate": "Hard inquiries occur when a lender or creditor pulls your credit report as part of a formal application for credit, such as applying for a mortgage, auto loan, or credit card. Soft inquiries, by contrast, happen when you check your own credit, when a company pre-screens you for a promotional offer, or when an employer conducts a background check. The key practical difference is that hard inquiries can temporarily lower your credit score, while soft inquiries have no impact on your score whatsoever. It's worth noting that the CFPB confirms that simply requesting your own credit reports will not hurt your credit score [2]. Multiple hard inquiries within a short window for the same type of loan (such as mortgage rate shopping) are often treated as a single inquiry by scoring models, which softens the impact. Hard inquiries typically remain visible on your credit report for two years, though their effect on your score generally fades after a few months. Being mindful of how often you formally apply for new credit is a useful habit, since a cluster of hard inquiries can signal financial stress to potential lenders.",
